Description: winebus udev backend: report the joystick buttons of the kernel's EV_BTN event
 The Linux input event EV_BTN (Tomasz Pakuła's "Input: New EV_BTN event for
 generic buttons", linux-input 2026-01-04, kernel series group 44 of the
 edition) numbers a joystick's buttons from 1 with no limit at KEY_MAX, which
 stops EV_KEY at 80 buttons. When the device offers EV_BTN and EVIOCGBTNCNT
 gives a count, the device's HID report holds that many buttons and an EV_BTN
 event sets button code - 1; EV_KEY is then ignored for the device, so a button
 is not counted twice. A device without EV_BTN is as before.
 This is the idea of Wine merge request 9853 (winebus: Add support for EV_BTN
 to udev, open draft, written for Wine master and in conflict with it) ported
 to the lnxev code of Wine 10.0. Differences from the merge request: the
 event bit is read and tested before the ioctl is used; the fallback
 EVIOCGBTNCNT is the real number, _IOR('E', 0x1c, unsigned int), not 0 (the
 ioctl 0 always fails, so a build against headers without EV_BTN never used the
 event); the event-bit buffer is sized (EV_MAX + 7) / 8; an event code outside
 1 to the button count is ignored; a device that has EV_BTN but gives no count
 is said on the error channel (the failed ioctl with its errno, or a count of 0) and keeps the EV_KEY path. Its second patch
 (BTN_GAMEPAD decides if a device is a gamepad) is left out.
Author: Daniel Ramos (Capitain Jack) <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Origin: other, https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/9853 (idea and first patch by Tomasz Pakuła)
Bug: https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/9853
Forwarded: no
